Supreme People's Court

Notice on Issuing the Rules for Judicial Police on Court Duty of the People's Courts

Fa Fa [2003] No. 13

To: the Higher People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army; and the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the Higher People's Court of the Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:

The Rules for Judicial Police on Court Duty of the People's Courts are hereby issued to you. Please organize serious study and implement them accordingly. In the event of any issues during implementation, please promptly report to our Court.

July 16, 2003

Rules for Judicial Police on Court Duty of the People's Courts

Article 1. These Rules are formulated in accordance with the relevant provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Court Rules of the People's Republic of China, and the Interim Regulations on Judicial Police of the People's Courts, for the purpose of ensuring the smooth progress of trial work of the People's Courts and standardizing the court duty activities of judicial police of the People's Courts.

Article 2. Court duty refers to the official acts performed by judicial police of the People's Courts during trial activities to maintain court order, ensure the safety of personnel participating in trial activities, and guarantee the smooth progress of trial activities.

Article 3. Judicial police on court duty shall perform their duties according to law during trial activities, subject to the directives of the presiding judge or the sole judge.

Article 4. The preparatory work before court duty shall be organized and implemented by the judicial police department:

(1) Select judicial police for court duty based on factors such as the time, scale, type of trial activity, number of litigation participants, and venue conditions;

(2) Formulate implementation plans and emergency measures for handling unexpected incidents;

(3) Contact relevant departments, exchange views, and clarify tasks.

Article 5. The duties of judicial police on court duty include:

(1) Guarding the court and maintaining court order;

(2) Ensuring the safety of personnel participating in trial activities;

(3) Summoning witnesses and expert witnesses;

(4) Receiving, passing, and presenting evidence;

(5) Stopping acts that disrupt trial activities.

Article 6. When on court duty, judicial police shall wear police uniforms and special insignia of police rank in accordance with regulations, and maintain a neat and proper appearance. Female judicial police shall not wear heavy makeup, have loose hair, or wear accessories.

Article 7. Judicial police on court duty shall be equipped with and use police implements and weapons in accordance with the provisions of the Regulations of the People's Police on the Use of Police Implements and Weapons.

Article 8. Judicial police on court duty shall conduct security checks on members of the audience. If minors, mentally ill persons, intoxicated persons, or other persons unsuitable for attending the trial are found, they shall prevent them from entering or persuade them to leave the courtroom.

Article 9. When on court duty, judicial police shall stand at the side of the judge's bench, with their backs to the bench and facing the audience gallery. They may adopt an attention stance, at-ease stance, or sitting posture as needed. They shall adopt an attention stance when the court pronounces judgment; they shall adopt a sitting posture after the court investigation begins. If court duty exceeds one hour, they may be relieved. When entering or exiting the courtroom, they shall march at a standard pace.

Article 10. When receiving, passing, or presenting evidence, judicial police on court duty shall pay attention to safety.

Article 11. When summoning witnesses, judicial police on court duty shall open the passage door and guide the witness to the designated location.

Article 12. Judicial police on court duty shall advise or stop members of the audience who commit any of the following violations of court discipline:

(1) Making audio recordings, taking photographs, or making video recordings without permission;

(2) Moving about at will or entering the court area without authorization;

(3) Applauding, making noise, or creating an uproar;

(4) Speaking or asking questions without authorization;

(5) Smoking or spitting on the floor;

(6) Using communication devices;

(7) Other acts violating court discipline.

Article 13. Judicial police on court duty may take compulsory measures according to law against the following acts:

(1) Entering the court area without permission, where advising or stopping is ineffective or where there is suspicion of illegal or criminal activity;

(2) Seriously violating court discipline, where advising or stopping is ineffective;

(3) Creating an uproar or storming the court, insulting, threatening, or assaulting personnel participating in trial activities, or other acts seriously disrupting court order.

Article 14. Compulsory measures that judicial police on court duty may take include: ordering to leave, forcibly escorting out, forcibly seizing, confiscating, inspecting, etc.

Article 15. Judicial police on court duty shall remain vigilant to prevent incidents such as self-harm, suicide, assault, or escape by the parties. In the event of an unexpected incident, they shall do their utmost, remain composed, and take decisive action.

Article 16. When on court duty, judicial police shall abide by court discipline, maintain concentration, behave with decorum, act civilly, and maintain a serious attitude. They shall not leave their posts without authorization, allow unrelated persons to contact the parties, insult or impose disguised corporal punishment on the parties, or engage in any other acts that disrupt trial activities.

Article 17. Judicial police who violate these Rules while on court duty shall be dealt with in accordance with the People's Police Law of the People's Republic of China.

Article 18. These Rules shall take effect from the date of issuance.
